Z||000019||Nashco||10-20-2001||02:37 AM||||Slammed:  383 without the 400 crank?  I imagine if you turned down the 350 crank journals enough with an offset centerpoint, you would get the same center point.  I'm not sure what the difference in stroke is between the two; perhaps you even have to build up a weld on one side and turn it down, but it *could* be done.  I don't know why you would want to do it that way, as it is much easier to just use the 400 crank.<P>Maybe his way involved a little stroke-machining of the crank AND some boring...?  I can't believe his book mentioned it, but he didn't clarify how, that's odd.  Anybody else have some thoughts on this?<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000020||Slammed Fiero||10-20-2001||10:15 AM||||I have been to a few mopar nationals with friends who are into the mark.<P>Every stock Hemi car there ran mid 13's.<P>Infact if you watch speedvision the one show is restoring a hemi challenger. They have yet to run it at the track , but there expecting mid 13's according to the engine builder.<P>In anycase... Where's chris moore? his dad has a Hemi something or other, all original.forget what it is.<P>JM ||||reg||1
