Z||000024||cadero2dmax||10-20-2001||03:47 PM||||Now, for disbelievers, my 84 Quad 4 was in the mid 13's with a 1.79 60' time (my personal best in that car).  <P>This was done at 70 degrees temp, 60% humidity, 800ft altitude. It was in full road racing trim, on Hoosier road racing slicks on all 4 corners.<P>What really amazes me is how many people that think big numbers in horsepower is what it is all about. If you are playing "my dog is bigger - - - ", maybe. But it is torque numbers that make the difference at the launch. Horsepower comes into play at the end when you are nearing redline.<P>What is the torque on the 383? probably close to 400lbft, perhaps? <P>My launch was due to the torque of that little 2.3 motor. Had I more hp than 238 on the end of the run, the V8's wouldn't be "slowly" catching up when we go through the traps (and I would have been in the 12's). <BR> <BR>Oh, BTW, that 383 should be in the high 11's or low 12's easily, on proper tires.<P>G||||reg||1
Z||000025||Pontiaddict||10-20-2001||07:48 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Immortal:<BR><B>The Mopar i spoke of was a 426 Cu 425 Hp HEMI stock. I smoked the tires the length of the 1/4 mile. I am not putting down fiero's. Just the times people say they turn with them.<P>Quicksand up to my neck by now.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'm assuming that you had one of these when they were new. There are plenty of reasons for you to believe what you do and I'll tell you why Fieros can run similar times with less motor.<P>First, 60's slicks were crap. Most were recapped and weren't really designed for traction beyond the point of having no tread for a larger contact patch. Tire compounds today are so much better that even the cheap, high treadwear rated tires, are better than 60's slicks.<P>Second, Fieros have excellent weight distribution for drag racing. It's 45/55 (approx) weight distribution is almost what drag racers were aiming for with altered wheelbase drag cars.<P>Third, engine technology has advanced so much that you can get more hp per displacement than you used to be able to. Newer cam, Intake, and cylinder head designs are tuned to make the car more driveable and still make plenty of horsepower.<P>Fourth, and this one is probably more opinion than anything, Stock Hemis aren't what they were craked up to be. Not that they were bad, but I'd have rather have had a 440.<P>Fifth, The Stock Hemi's HP rating, was in gross hp. While EFI makes some of the same changes that the car compaines used to do to skew the HP numbers in their favor, there is still about a 20% difference between a gross rated musclecar motor and a new high tech EFI car due to the gross rated motor not having an exhaust or any accessories accounting for parasitic losses. If your 425hp Hemi was net rated, it would make about 340 hp.<P>I see why you believe what you do, but there is an explaination for why you can get a Fiero into the 12's.||||reg||1
Z||000026||Spektrum-87GT||10-20-2001||07:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Immortal:<BR><B>12.9 in 1/4 mile with street tires NO WAY. Even with a SBC in it.<P>Are you sure it was not a 1/8th mile track ?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>uhh.... i dont know what to say about this. slammed_fiero is right on everything he's said. go down to your local track and watch, i'm sure you'll see little cars there running 12s on street tires. until then, stfu, jeesh, im sick of the moronic things i hear on this forum.||||reg||1
Z||000027||Formula88||10-20-2001||08:11 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Immortal:<BR><B>The Mopar i spoke of was a 426 Cu 425 Hp HEMI stock. I smoked the tires the length of the 1/4 mile. I am not putting down fiero's. Just the times people say they turn with them.<P>Quicksand up to my neck by now.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Dude, first off - welcome to the forum.  Second, I LOVE HEMI's YES!  <P>Third, Tina has time slips to prove she ran 12.9s.  Ease up.  Just because you didn't think it could be done doesn't mean someone else hasn't already done it.  V8 Archie has a link on one of his pages to a Fiero setup for drag racing. V8 swap with an automatic tranny and it runs 10s.  Not sure about the tires, though.  You can stuff some serious meat under a Fiero with the right rims, and with the engine over the drive wheels, you get better traction than in a front engine, RWD muscle car.  Food for thought.<P>Take it easy.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000028||Nashco||10-20-2001||10:11 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Pontiaddict:<BR><B>Third, engine technology has advanced so much that you can get more hp per displacement than you used to be able to. Newer cam, Intake, and cylinder head designs are tuned to make the car more driveable and still make plenty of horsepower.<P>Fourth, and this one is probably more opinion than anything, Stock Hemis aren't what they were craked up to be. Not that they were bad, but I'd have rather have had a 440.<P>Fifth, The Stock Hemi's HP rating, was in gross hp. While EFI makes some of the same changes that the car compaines used to do to skew the HP numbers in their favor, there is still about a 20% difference between a gross rated musclecar motor and a new high tech EFI car due to the gross rated motor not having an exhaust or any accessories accounting for parasitic losses. If your 425hp Hemi was net rated, it would make about 340 hp.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ok, I totally agree with your first couple points, but as a strong hemi supporter, I have to stick up for it.<P>First, I have to argue with your third point.  Engine technology nowadays strives for what the hemi started.  I'm sure you know how the hemi head valve configuration is, as that is what makes it a hemi.  It has a hemispherical head chamber, huge valves opposing each other, and the spark plug fires in the direct center of the combustion chamber.  Modern engines today aim to achieve these kinds of combustion chamber dynamics with multiple valves per cylinder.  Imports came around first by going with overhead cams and spark plugs going through the valve cover, as I'm sure you've seen.  As far as the intake, the weber sixpack was not having any problems delivering fuel; with six barrels (operating progressively) to deliver from, tuning capabilities were amazing.  Between being able to run higher compression than other head designs, and providing enough fuel, the only thing you had to do was flow some air through via headers and a tall intake, and you had massive power available.<P>On to your fourth point...stock hemis aren't what they're cracked up to be?  Stock hemis are arguably one of the most performance oriented engines made before the mid 80's.  The 440 was almost as well of a performer, but getting more power out of the hemi was much easier with better durability.  Was there any vehicle at the time that was faster than the fastest hemi equipped car?  Yes, it was all it was cracked up to be, because it was the best there was out of the box.  I wish there weren't so many owners back then that said, "I'd rather have a 440," simply because their factory numbers were almost the same for a little less money.  The hemi has some awesome potential, and those who tapped into it ruled the dragstrip.<P>Fifth point...although they did use gross power, rather than net, the hemi was rated much lower than it really was.  There are several magazines I've read that claimed their hemi equipped cars got a touch over 400 horse at the wheel as stock.  If you have, say, 410 horse at the wheel, you can assume the vehicle has at least 450 at the motor.  Some say that the factory claimed lower numbers than what the actual amount was to avoid problems with regulationary offices.<P>Now, I don't have any problem with you, I'm just saying, don't slam the hemi.  The hemi started technology that we all wish we had in our engines TODAY.  I am very bitter towards NASCAR, because they banned the hemi equipped daytona/superbird design.  Rather than making the competitor's car step up to this newly drawn line, they regulated the competition so "things would be fair."  I don't understand why they would put developments like this down.  Aerodynamics and good engineering were apparently not on their list of to-dos.  There are claims that this was for safety reasons, but I think it would have been easy to make a safer car.<P>The hemi powered cars still hold records at race tracks for having the fastest average lap times.  Why not bring them back, if they're so good?  Well, Dodge had a huge amount of engineering going into a redesigned hemi that was to be released in 2002 Chrysler 300s and 2003 Dodge Charger R/Ts...but the Germans put that to an end, thanks guys.  It looked to be an awesome motor, but apparently it wasn't beneficial profits-wise enough to make it worth their while.<P>Hemi motors have an unbelievable amount of power on tap, and that's why they dominate many racing circuits.  Drag racing in particular, has a lot of front-runners using hemi motors.  Many monster trucks use hemis as well, because of their awesome torque pounds per engine pound numbers.  I wish that kind of head design was used on our engines, as performance would have to increase at least 20%, not to mention the things that we could do from there.<P>I have no argument against fast fieros, as I do know they exits.  I doubt, though, that any fiero chassis will ever go as fast as the fastest hemi-equipped car chassis has been.<P>In summation, don't slam the hemi, as it is what we should aspire to have.  Just as the northstar, tdc, and quad four have things to desire for us, so does a hemi design.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<P>Edited for typo<p>[This message has been edited by Nashco (edited 10-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000029||Pontiaddict||10-21-2001||12:17 AM||||Nashco, I was going to counter your points, but it was mainly a long drawn out thing saying that except for a few points, I agree with you. <P>So I'll just say that the Hemi had lots of potential, (4 second top fuelers come to mind.) but as they came from the factory, my preferance would still be a 440. My point about Hp ratings was more about how technology has advanced than how this motor or that one is better than a Hemi. {BTW they still underrate cars, the F-body for example makes about as much HP at the wheels as it's factory rated to make at the crank.}<P>I'll just leave it at that.||||reg||1
Z||000030||Slammed Fiero||10-21-2001||12:28 AM||||Nashco is actually dead on , on the HP.<P>I don't know how many of you get Dream Car Garage on Speedvision. But there rebuilding a hemi challenger. The one episode had the freshly rebuilt motor on the dyno ...453hp at the flywheel.<P>But back to Fiero's...  A Fiero with a 300Hp v8 (ie Lt1) is faster than 99% of the muscle cars from the 60's with the exception of a few. I believe there were a handful of "Silver Bullet" hemi belvedere's (sp)  built that were mid 12's , and Also a bunch of one off stuff like COPO camaro's , yenko camaro's.<P>The problem wasn't there power , but the means of delivery , ie tire technology that sucked , we have seen street cars on 10" DOT slicks run in the 9's if not faster.  Cars were heavy back then , HP was rated Differently.<P>It's all about power to weight ratio and how you put it on the ground. I have dragged a Few vettes with 2 times the HP of my weiner 2.8 , I always get a huge hole shot..<P>God Bless mid / Rear engined cars.. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>JM||||reg||1
Z||000031||Immortal||10-21-2001||01:17 AM||||NASHCO:<P>That HEMI I spoke of did ont have a six pack it had 2 four barrels carbs.<P>As I said I am not knocking fiero's. I have an 87 and 86. The only reason for good hole shots is RPM ie short stroke. Good starts but cant sustain the torque.<P><BR>I am not to far from where the hold the Keysyone Nationals I've seen alot of cars run there. Never seen a fiero run there. <P>but I could be wrong?||||reg||1
Z||000032||California Kid||10-21-2001||11:51 AM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by Formula88:<BR>[B] <BR>Third, Tina has time slips to prove she ran 12.9s.  Ease up.  Just because you didn't think it could be done doesn't mean someone else hasn't already done it.  V8 Archie has a link on one of his pages to a Fiero setup for drag racing. V8 swap with an automatic tranny and it runs 10s.  Not sure about the tires, though.  You can stuff some serious meat under a Fiero with the right rims, and with the engine over the drive wheels, you get better traction than in a front engine, RWD muscle car.  Food for thought.<BR>*********************************************<BR>HHmmmmmmmm...Guess Immortal wouldn't believe a 12.2 quarter with a 400hp small block, 5spd, and BFG Comp T/A's 245x50x16.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/engine_8.jpg"> <P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature_7.jpg"> ||||reg||1
